 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A conjunction joins words, phrases or clauses.\n• Coordinating conjunctions join equal parts: and (adds), but (contrasts), or (choice), so (result), for, nor, yet. “I like films, but my brother prefers music.”\n• Subordinating conjunctions introduce a dependent clause: because, since (reason); although, though (contrast); when, while, after, before, until (time); if, unless (condition); so that (purpose).\n“We stayed inside because it was raining.” “Although she was tired, she danced all night.”\n• Correlative pairs: both … and, either … or, neither … nor, not only … but also.\nA comma is used after a subordinate clause at the start: “When the film ended, we went home.”",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "Join with a suitable conjunction: “The concert was cancelled. The singer was ill.”",
  "steps": [
   "The second sentence gives the reason for the first.",
   "A reason is introduced by “because”: The concert was cancelled because the singer was ill."
  ],
  "answer": "The concert was cancelled because the singer was ill.",
  "checks": []
 },
